// Runtime/ProcessRuntime.maxon — the process family's COMPILER-SIDE VOCABULARY: the three entry-point names
// the parser emits calls to, the band test that decides which targets may run them, and the ONE body still
// built as IR here.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT THE FAMILY SERVES — THREE `__Builtins` INTRINSICS ABOUT THE RUNNING PROCESS.**
// `stdlib/Process.maxon`'s `executablePath()` bottoms out in `__Builtins.executablePath()`;
// `Compiler/TreeLock.maxon`'s lock token bottoms out in `__Builtins.currentProcessId()`; and
// `Main.runSpecTest` calls `__Builtins.enterBackgroundPriority()` before it spawns a worker pool. Only the
// first has a stdlib wrapper: a declaration whose whole body is one intrinsic call is the thin wrapper the
// house rules forbid. Nothing else in the language reaches these entry points.
//
// ⛔⛔ **TWO OF THE THREE BODIES ARE RUNTIME SOURCE AND THIS ONE IS NOT, AND THE LINE IS A MECHANISM RATHER
// THAN AN APPETITE.** `runtime/Process.maxon` writes `__proc_pid` and `__proc_bg_priority`, each one `__Raw`
// op the tier can spell. `__proc_exe_path` cannot follow yet: it allocates a raw buffer, grows it and builds
// an `__ManagedMemory` answer, so as tier source it would CALL other runtime entries — and `scanRuntimeUsage`
// walks every runtime body in every program, so a call inside one would set that family's bit for a program
// dead-function elimination sweeps the body out of. That vacuity holds only while every tier body calls
// nothing but `__Raw`, which names no callee. The allocator's own migration is what answers it.
//
// ⭐ **TWO HOST FACILITIES, AND WHICH TARGETS HAVE EACH IS `TargetFacilities`' TO SAY.** A target that has
// neither refuses at the call site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE `__proc_` PREFIX DOES NOT ANSWER FOR THE WHOLE BAND.** The two IDENTITY reads answer
// `processInfo` by that prefix; the PRIORITY write answers `processPriority`, which `calleeHostFacility`
// tests for by NAME ahead of the band. The split records a difference in KIND rather than a schedule:
// reading an identity is one call that cannot fail, while the write's POSIX scope disagrees —
// `setpriority` scopes to the process on Darwin and to the calling THREAD on Linux — so a lane can serve
// the reads without serving the write, and one answer for three entries would make the whole band wait on
// the narrowest of them. ⇒ **A fourth entry point is still covered by construction, and it will answer
// `processInfo`** — which is right for another identity read and wrong for another write, so a write joins
// by name.
//
// ⚠ The executable-path fill is a Std loop rather than a lowering because the three OS APIs
// disagree on their own shape. `stdlib/Process.maxon` names all three — `GetModuleFileNameA` on
// Windows, `_NSGetExecutablePath` on macOS, `/proc/self/exe` on Linux. The first fills a caller's
// buffer and answers a count; the second takes an in-out size, signals "too small" through a `-1`
// return plus a rewritten size, leaves that size unchanged on success (so the length must be
// measured) and answers a path that "may be a symbolic link and not the real file"; the third is a
// symlink that must be `readlink`'d and is not NUL-terminated. The Std tier is target-neutral, so
// each backend translates its own convention into `osExecutablePath`'s and the one retry loop,
// `ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitGrowingPathFill`, serves all three. wasm is refused for a stronger
// reason: WASI has no executable-path primitive at all. A component has no image path to report,
// and `args_get[0]` is whatever the host chose to pass rather than a resolved path — so a lowering
// there could only be a plausible wrong answer, which is worse than a refusal because `File.exists`
// on it would simply say false and the program would blame itself.

// The `__proc_` prefix that identifies every process runtime entry point, and the one `SemanticCheck` reads
// for E3104 through `TargetFacilities.calleeHostFacility`.
let ProcessRuntimePrefix = b"__proc_"

// ⭐ **THE THREE ENTRY POINTS.** Only the first is built below; the other two are declared in
// `runtime/Process.maxon` and reached by a call the parser emits from the `__Builtins` spelling.
export let ProcessExecutablePathName = b"__proc_exe_path"
export let ProcessCurrentPidName = b"__proc_pid"

// ⭐ **THE ONE THE PREFIX DOES NOT ANSWER FOR.** It is refused by E3104 on a target that cannot lower
// it, but through `processPriority` rather than `processInfo` — see the header for why a WRITE whose scope
// differs per platform cannot be fused to the two identity reads.
export let ProcessBackgroundPriorityName = b"__proc_bg_priority"

// `__proc_exe_path() -> arrRec`: a fresh owned `__ManagedMemory` holding the running image's
// absolute path, which the caller frees.
//
// A refused fill answers the empty buffer rather than throwing, the intrinsic's documented contract
// (`/specs/builtins-type.md`: "empty buffer when unavailable").
//
// `_NSGetExecutablePath` rewrites its in-out size to the one it needs, and `osExecutablePath`
// carries no channel for that number: a channel only one lane can fill would make this
// target-neutral graph ask which target it compiles for. That lane answers "too small" and pays at
// most one extra doubling (`Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinExePathChunk`).
//
// The raw buffer is a scratch allocation this function owns end to end: it is freed on the one
// exit, and what the caller receives is the independent `__ManagedMemory` copy.
function buildProcessExecutablePath(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ProcessExecutablePathName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var attempt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxattempt", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fitTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxfit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxgrow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unavailable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var copyOut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pxcopy", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let (buffer, _) = emitGrowingPathFill(module, cur: entry, attempt: attempt, fitTest: fitTest, grow: grow, fill: PathFill.executablePath, whenRefused: unavailable, whenFits: copyOut, ids: ids)

	// Unavailable: make the buffer the empty string and take the ordinary copy-out.
	let emptyAt = emitConst(module, block: unavailable, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyByte = emitConst(module, block: unavailable, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: unavailable, base: buffer, index: emptyAt, value: emptyByte, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unavailable, op: StdOp.branch(copyOut.id))

	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: copyOut, cstr: buffer, record: record, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: built, ptr: buffer, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildProcessExecutablePath'

// ⚠ **THE GUARD IS PER ENTRY, NOT PER FILE**, and one entry is left to guard: the path walk drags in
// `usesHeap` AND `usesManagedMemory`, i.e. the whole managed-memory runtime installed as one closure, so a
// program whose only introspection is a pid must not reach it. The other two entries need no guard here at
// all — their bodies are tier source, and dead-function elimination decides whether each survives.
export function installProcessRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesExecutablePath 'executablePath'
		buildProcessExecutablePath(module)
	end 'executablePath'
end 'installProcessRuntime'

// ⭐ **THE FAMILY'S BAND, AND IT ANSWERS FOR THE `processInfo` HALF.** `calleeHostFacility` reads it AFTER
// its by-name test for the priority write, so the write never reaches here. A PREFIX test rather than an
// `equals` against a roster of names, for `isCommandLineRuntimeCallee`'s reason: a further identity read is
// then covered by construction instead of having to be remembered at two sites.
// `specs/process-id.md:rejected-on-wasm` is the case that pins the E3104 `__proc_pid` earns by prefix alone.
//
// ⚠ **`scanRuntimeUsage` DOES NOT READ THIS ONE.** It asks for the path walk by NAME, because a prefix is
// the right test for "may this target run it at all" — one answer for the family — and the wrong test for
// "must this program carry it", which is per entry point and costs wildly different amounts here.
export function isProcessRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ProcessRuntimePrefix)
end 'isProcessRuntimeCallee'
